#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083056 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "879e41c3-7d67-5821-81dc-f6d3fc664ffd" :question/math-academy-id "e-597" :question/problem "Suppose that we have a curve defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2t+3, \\quad y=t^3-4t, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $t=-1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2(-1)+3=1, \\\\ y & =(-1)^{3}-4(-1)=-1+4=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(1,3).$ The plot below shows the curve and the tangent line at this point.\n\n![](images/0f/0fa3fc12e83e2a581499680145df9229917c7426e6a3d12c51d88e115a42033e.png)\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2, \\qquad y'(t) = 3t^2-4 .\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)} = \\dfrac{3t^2-4}{2}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=-1} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}-4}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $(x_1,y_1) = (1,3)$ and the slope $m=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\ny - 3 = -\\frac{1}{2}\\left(x-1\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083057 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "898e04b8-1b7e-59e7-b03f-3ece54aa093b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Normal Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "db8c5a47-27b8-548d-8bd7-65289cff06da" :question/math-academy-id "e-598" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9dcc4c97bd296ef4627b02b6cfafcdb643f475cd8dacd36a820db760bd6fb045.png)\n\nThe following parametric equations define the curve shown above:\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos{t} +\\sin{2t},\\quad y=\\cos{t} - 2\\sin{2t},\\qquad 0\\leq t<2\\pi\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ (shown as a dashed line in the plot above)." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}+1, \\\\  \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $\\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right).$ For this, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = -2\\sin t + 2\\cos 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -\\sin t - 4 \\cos 2t .\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2}+4(0)}{2(0-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} = -2\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the normal line, we use the point-slope form \n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m_n(x-x_1),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) = \\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right), \\qquad m_n=-2,\n$$\n\nand we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2) & =-2(x-(\\sqrt{2}+1)) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+2 & =-2x+2\\sqrt{2}+2 \\\\ y+2x & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083074 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083278 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/id #uuid "72076ddb-4a3a-530f-854c-7b1400cd9859" :question/math-academy-id "e-6240" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=t^2+3, \\quad y=-t+1, \\qquad -\\infty <     t <    \\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if $x'(t)=0$ while $y'(t) \\neq 0 .$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -1 .\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n2t=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $y'(0) = -1\\neq 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t = 0,$ the tangent to the curve is vertical."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083075 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/id #uuid "b2f8d797-75e8-5ac8-82e7-1c5a9788e6e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-6252" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2t^3-1,\\quad y=t^2-t+1,\\qquad-\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the **normal** to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The normal is horizontal if the tangent is vertical. Therefore, we require $x'(t)=0$ while $y'(t) \\neq 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n6t^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =2\\cdot 0-1=-1\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t = 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38170285-5909-5a87-b81e-7614b331e561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254153} {:answer/id #uuid "61b95ad1-c677-5a1d-9693-7fe23f0898f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254545} {:answer/id #uuid "74ee3251-ef46-57f3-8565-b37dbcb4bbe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm 3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254706} {:answer/id #uuid "9f808726-09d3-5692-a435-5070f152cddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255075} {:answer/id #uuid "cd387a61-32a4-5106-b17f-5c138d6c2ae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd387a61-32a4-5106-b17f-5c138d6c2ae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255494} :answer-field/id #uuid "7461ea8a-3c02-5867-96b2-21f3b9f03a9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e5d7b42-493e-5694-92f6-e6ea1c2f15fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-122946" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7(t-1),\\,y=2{t}^{3}-18t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the points at which the normal to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=7,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6({t}^{2}-3)=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(-\\sqrt{3}) & =7\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\sqrt{3}) & =7\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "898e04b8-1b7e-59e7-b03f-3ece54aa093b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Normal Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1129c14f-0c46-52c4-9b9a-b4200ce4b285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272542} {:answer/id #uuid "5e5faf98-bf15-5a2b-9fdc-09a9a727c6b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273199} {:answer/id #uuid "5f37ca8d-3ea5-5dc1-85e2-3a3270cb4ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273206} {:answer/id #uuid "7e745226-ecb3-5bd0-91a4-532e194cf6a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273471} {:answer/id #uuid "818af87a-0a7b-5074-95df-e9381f58f773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1129c14f-0c46-52c4-9b9a-b4200ce4b285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272542} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb3ec910-ef36-5928-896c-acb859df3e11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbab7bcf-6a8b-5a33-ae85-61d0acf13fd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-15570" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,,\\,t\\in (0,\\pi ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]=\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0, \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /2} & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(-1)\\,}=1\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083278 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02788049-040e-5c76-86e2-58bb6a5bbbef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284808} {:answer/id #uuid "5833d2bf-74d0-5ea2-94bc-f893f8489ff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285598} {:answer/id #uuid "98173c7f-8ba4-5f06-88fe-3425d308e469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286180} {:answer/id #uuid "ab587a99-40a5-5596-90ea-b5a5e96c6204" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286350} {:answer/id #uuid "e27e9371-7143-528d-820d-b073e0dc0b31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02788049-040e-5c76-86e2-58bb6a5bbbef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284808} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f6ee0e5-3926-5962-8761-13aa6562ef64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6e05e4c-c6f0-521f-b67c-d81eff855827" :question/math-academy-id "q-20853" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2(t+1),\\,y=4{t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n8t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that ${x}^{′}(0)=2\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t=0,$ the tangent to the curve is horizontal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0735403-c4cc-5f1c-b3f3-f20e814b4e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439583} {:answer/id #uuid "18c5b8fc-dc2a-5f66-aba9-2fb2139b0e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18c5b8fc-dc2a-5f66-aba9-2fb2139b0e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439584} :answer-field/id #uuid "45f84d85-7a93-543f-94bb-a475c80511cd" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440149} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7325a515-140f-5d60-bed7-46dc88237008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439585} {:answer/id #uuid "76c0b0ff-3576-50aa-ae9a-450fb61d13ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76c0b0ff-3576-50aa-ae9a-450fb61d13ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439586} :answer-field/id #uuid "23d3ed3a-04d8-5745-91e6-80f3e4c230a7" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440150} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a631b20-d142-5c85-8a98-edc055f0d25f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439587} {:answer/id #uuid "2b5cc863-692b-5dad-a794-3874acf00aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a631b20-d142-5c85-8a98-edc055f0d25f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439587} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc1b0da1-c6f8-5d48-bed9-a7f7760363df" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440151}] :question/id #uuid "57cb918d-01c6-5793-92dd-c253fd54156c" :question/math-academy-id "q-21080" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n$x = 4\\cos 2t + 5, y = 5\\sin 2t + 4, 0 \\le t < π$.\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is horizontal.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $t=0$\n- **II.** $t=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$\n- **III.** $t=\\pi$" :question/worked-solution "A vertical tangent (and horizontal normal) requires x′(t)=0 and y′(t)≠0. Here $x′(t)=- 8 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}$ and $y′(t)=10 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}$. Within the given half-open interval the solutions are $\\left\\{0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right\\}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083278 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12952b5e-889f-54cc-a122-5d50215660f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12952b5e-889f-54cc-a122-5d50215660f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317625} :answer-field/id #uuid "162eb28c-9915-5e71-854b-15570d6140d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318471} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc186f89-e80b-5eb4-a0f9-50d2a3804e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc186f89-e80b-5eb4-a0f9-50d2a3804e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318301} :answer-field/id #uuid "45248884-e4ee-5dd3-b87d-9707883c9d1c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8ca39289-4112-5812-a40f-16d38e719123" :question/math-academy-id "q-350359" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,y={t}^{3}+7{t}^{2}+8t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+14t+8\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}+14t+8 & =0 \\\\ (3t+2)(t+4) & =0 \\\\ t & =-4,-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(-4) & =2(-4)+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{15}{2}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(-\\frac{2}{3}) & =2(-\\frac{2}{3})+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{5}{6}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-4$ and $t=-\\frac{2}{3}.$"}]}
